  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + z²)/2, drawn as a surface at 0.4 × loss, so the left-right direction is steeper. Each step moves by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Above a learning rate of 0.8, the left-right swings grow.",
    "Noise per slope component has standard deviation 2·√((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch size B: 2 at B=1 and exactly 0 at B=64 (the whole simulated set). It is centred on the true slope.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 2 × the true step length (learning rate × slope estimate). The step-length readout gives the true value. Grey: the step with the full-data slope. Orange: the step the ball will actually take.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged once the loss passes 8. The ball is held on the drawn surface, but the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence, and each new run uses the next noise values. The trails hold the start point plus up to 40 steps.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average. With small batches, a single step can raise it."
  ]
}